WEBVTT

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.980
Recently, it was announced that NASA is planning to send a crewed mission back to NASA.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.960
the Moon and to Mars. Obviously, huge technological challenges will need to be overcome before

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:41.160
these missions can be successfully accomplished. NASA researchers realize that trips like these

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.160
will require building the appropriate type of spacecraft, having flawless life support systems,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.460
and will need the right tools to perform work once we arrive on these distant worlds.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.940
But a major concern that often gets overlooked by the general public is what types of food will

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.270
be eaten by our astronauts on these long missions, fortunately.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.330
Previous missions to low Earth orbit in the space shuttle and longer missions aboard the

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:09.250
International Space Station have helped NASA better understand how food and the astronaut

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:09.770
interact.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.570
But not much is known about how food will fare on these long missions.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.480
The primary goal of the food systems in these long missions will be to provide a palatable,

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.900
nutritious and safe food for our explorers, while also taking up as little room as possible.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.100
Food is vital for survival here on Earth, but is even more important in some respects

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:29.580
in space.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:36.530
Its preparation, quantity, and quality are critical and can affect astronauts on a physiological level.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.420
One of the most crucial problems on long missions is bone loss.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.980
Typically, astronauts lose 1-2% of bone mass each month that they are in space,

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.560
especially in the lower halves of their bodies.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.540
In the weightless environment of space, there is almost no stress on the skeletal system.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.050
Bones are no longer providing support to walk and are not being used to maintain body posture.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:01:59.590
This

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.320
This lack of stress on the bones may be a key factor in an astronaut's progressive

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.520
bone loss in space.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.770
Other problems like fluid shift and space motion sickness must be taken into account

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.890
when providing food to the astronaut crews.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.610
Meals must be chosen that can help slow many of the problems faced by astronauts.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.160
Another major concern for NASA food scientists is the stability of food that is packaged

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:23.980
for these missions.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.440
It's vital that the food remain edible for years at a time, staying safe and stable

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:29.860
aboard.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.950
spacecraft. This is perhaps one of the most important factors of the planned long-duration

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:40.600
missions. If the food spoils, there are no options currently available to astronauts for nourishment.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:46.490
With missions to Mars requiring at least three years to complete, stored food must remain shelf

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.950
stable for that time, preferably longer. In the short term, food systems that are currently being

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:57.280
used aboard the shuttle and space station are suitable for transit to another world. But once

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.960
Once astronauts arrive, other alternatives need to be...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.410
With these thoughts in mind, researchers at NASA are developing new ways to help crews eat well in space.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:12.130
In addition to storing food aboard the spacecraft, many at NASA believe that growing food in space,

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:17.510
or on planetary surfaces, will need to be perfected to help feed astronauts on these long missions.
